---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
import Photo from '../../components/Photo.astro';
import HowItsMade from '../../components/HowItsMade.astro';
import { brand } from '../../lib/config';
export const prerender = true;
const article = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'Poolish bread: what it is and why it ferments for up to 35 hours',
  description:
    'What poolish is, how it transforms bread and how it differs from sourdough.',
  inLanguage: 'en',
  mainEntityOfPage: brand.site
    ? new URL('/en/poolish', brand.site).href
    : undefined,
  author: {
    '@id': brand.site ? new URL('/#organization', brand.site).href : undefined,
  },
  publisher: {
    '@id': brand.site ? new URL('/#organization', brand.site).href : undefined,
  },
  dateModified: '2026-11-23',
  image: brand.site
    ? new URL('/images/crumb-1100.webp', brand.site).href
    : undefined,
};
---

<Article
  lang="en"
  route="poolish"
  crumb="Poolish"
  title={`Poolish or sourdough: what it is and how they differ · ${brand.name}`}
  description="What poolish is, why the bread ferments for up to 35 hours and how it differs from sourdough. Explained by a small artisan bakery in Sant Boi."
  kicker="Slow fermentation"
  h1="Poolish bread: a night’s head start."
  lede="What poolish is, what it does to the loaf and how it is similar to (and different from) sourdough."
  jsonLd={[article]}
>
  <div class="article-grid">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What poolish is</h2>
      <p>
        It’s a pre-dough, or pre-ferment: flour and water in equal parts with a
        very small amount of yeast. It has the texture of thick cream and is
        made the day before. While it rests overnight, the yeast works slowly
        and the flour develops aromas that quick kneading has no time to create.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then the poolish is mixed with the rest of the flour, water and salt.
        The dough is folded by hand, each loaf is shaped and it ferments another
        night in the cold before going into the oven. All in all, up to 35
        hours.
      </p>

      <h2>What you notice in the loaf</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Flavour</strong>: deeper, slightly sweet and toasty.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Crumb</strong>: more open, moist and elastic.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Crust</strong>: thin, dark golden and crunchy.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Keeping</strong>: it stays soft for more days than a quickly
          fermented bread.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Poolish or sourdough?</h2>
      <p>
        Both are slow fermentations, but they are not the same. Sourdough is a
        culture of wild yeasts and lactic bacteria that is fed every day; it
        makes breads with a distinct sour note. Poolish uses yeast, but very
        little of it and with plenty of time: the result is a bread with
        character and an open crumb, with a milder flavour and no sourness.
      </p>
      <p>
        I make both, and I’d rather be clear about it: the weekly loaf is
        <strong>poolish</strong>, and there is also
        <a href="/en/#carta">sourdough bread</a> on the menu. If you’re after a
        slow-fermented bread with a friendly taste for every day, poolish is
        exactly that; if you like a sour note, choose sourdough.
      </p>

      <h2>What a bake looks like</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Until closing</strong> (usually two days before, at 20:00): I
          close orders.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>After closing</strong>: I make the poolish, which rests all
          night.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Then</strong>: I knead with folds, shape each loaf by hand and
          leave it to ferment another night in the cold.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Oven</strong> and then{' '}
          <a href="/en/bread-delivery">to your door</a> on Wednesday or
          Saturday, or <a href="/en/pickup">pickup in Sant Boi</a>.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <div class="article-photos">
      <figure class="article-photo">
        <Photo
          name="crumb"
          alt="Open crumb of a poolish loaf with a toasted crust"
          sizes="(max-width: 860px) calc(100vw - 40px), 40vw"
        />
      </figure>
      <figure class="article-photo">
        <Photo
          name="dough"
          alt="Shaped poolish doughs resting in floured proofing baskets"
          sizes="(max-width: 860px) calc(100vw - 40px), 40vw"
        />
      </figure>
    </div>
  </div>
  <HowItsMade lang="en" slot="after" />
</Article>
